  "id": "COMPOUND:2294 - PROTEIN:4951",
  "verbalization": "Geldanamycin, etidronate, and sitostanone all share a common mechanism of action by targeting DNA-directed RNA polymerase subunit beta, a key enzyme involved in RNA synthesis. This enzyme is a critical component of the transcription process, and its inhibition can disrupt gene expression, potentially leading to cellular dysfunction. The observed relationship between these compounds and HL-60 cells, a human promyelocytic leukemia cell line, suggests that the inhibition of DNA-directed RNA polymerase subunit beta may contribute to the antileukemic activity of these agents. The evidence from the visible paths indicates that these compounds are effective in modulating the expression of genes in HL-60 cells, which may be relevant to their therapeutic potential. The consistent targeting of this enzyme across different compounds supports a mechanistic link between the drugs and the disease, though the exact downstream effects on cell viability or apoptosis remain to be fully elucidated.",
